    {'title': 'FastAPI & Modern Backend Architecture: ASGI Architecture, Event Loops & Uvicorn Worker Model (Pattern #1)', 'slug': 'fastapi-asgi-01-asgi-architecture,-event-loops', 'category': 'fastapi_architecture', 'difficulty': 'basic', 'phase': 'basic', 'min_exp_years': 1, 'max_exp_years': 3, 'company_tags': ['Netflix', 'Meta', 'Uber'], 'frequency_score': 93, 'mindmap_topic': 'fastapi-architecture', 'mindmap_node': 'fastapi', 'scenario_text': 'In a FastAPI microservice serving 15,000 API requests per second, engineers diagnosed latency spikes or threadpool exhaustion related to ASGI Architecture, Event Loops & Uvicorn Worker Model. Analyze the ASGI request lifecycle and implement a non-blocking, clean architecture solution.', 'theory_explanation': '### 💡 In Simple Words (The Intuition)\nThink of FastAPI\'s async event loop as a single master chef: if the chef stops to chop onions for 5 minutes, all orders wait. Delegate chopping to helpers in the threadpool!\n\n### ⚙️ Under The Hood (Why It Happens)\nFastAPI runs `async def` endpoints directly on the single-threaded asyncio event loop. If code inside `async def` performs synchronous blocking I/O (e.g. `requests.get` or `time.sleep`), the entire server freezes.\n\n### 🚀 Step-by-Step Production Fix\n1. Use standard `def` for synchronous/blocking functions (FastAPI automatically runs them in a worker threadpool).\n2. For async code, use asynchronous libraries (`httpx.AsyncClient`, `asyncpg`).\n3. Leverage `yield` dependencies for clean database session commit/close handling.\n\n### 🎯 30-Second MNC Interview Punchline\n*"Never run synchronous blocking I/O inside `async def`; use `def` for threadpool offloading or purely non-blocking async drivers."*', 'cross_questions': ['What is the difference between defining a route as `async def` vs `def` in FastAPI?', "How does FastAPI's dependency injection system handle `yield` dependencies during client disconnection?", 'Why does wrapping streaming responses in BaseHTTPMiddleware cause memory buffering issues?'], 'common_traps': ['Calling synchronous blocking libraries (`requests`, `time.sleep`, synchronous DB drivers) inside `async def`.', 'Using BackgroundTasks for mission-critical jobs that must survive server restarts.'], 'best_practices': ['Use `async def` only with truly non-blocking awaitable drivers.', 'Use Pydantic v2 `@field_validator` and `@model_validator` for high-speed Rust-based payload validation.'], 'mcq_question': 'What happens if you execute a blocking synchronous database query inside a FastAPI endpoint declared with `async def`?', 'mcq_options': ['A) FastAPI automatically spawns a new OS process to isolate the blocking code.', 'B) It freezes the entire event loop, preventing all concurrent requests from making progress.', 'C) It raises a RuntimeError immediately.', 'D) The query is automatically converted to an asynchronous coroutine.'], 'mcq_correct_index': 1, 'mcq_explanation': 'Declaring an endpoint `async def` means FastAPI runs it directly on the main event loop thread; blocking synchronous calls prevent the loop from processing other concurrent tasks.', 'has_python_sandbox': True, 'has_sql_sandbox': False, 'has_redis_sandbox': False, 'has_nosql_sandbox': False, 'starter_code': "from fastapi import FastAPI, Depends\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# Non-blocking async route\n@app.get('/health')\nasync def health():\n    return {'status': 'healthy'}\n", 'solution_code': "from fastapi import FastAPI, Depends\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# Non-blocking async route\n@app.get('/health')\nasync def health():\n    return {'status': 'healthy'}\n", 'test_cases_json': []},
